      "text": "To find the sum of the \\( x \\)-values that satisfy the equation \\( 5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2} \\), we start by simplifying the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, we perform polynomial long division or synthetic division on the numerator \\( x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x \\) by the denominator \\( x + 2 \\).\n\n1. **Synthetic Division:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r|rrrr}\n     -2 & 1 & -2 & -8 & 0 \\\\\n        &   & -2 & 8 & 0 \\\\\n     \\hline\n        & 1 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   The result of the division is:\n\n   \\[\n   x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = (x + 2)(x^2 - 4x + 0)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Simplify the fraction:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2} = \\frac{(x + 2)(x^2 - 4x)}{x + 2} = x^2 - 4x\n   \\]\n\n3. **Form the equation:**\n\n   Given \\( 5 = x^2 - 4x \\), we set this equal to zero:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 4x = 5\n   \\]\n\n   Rearrange the equation to the standard quadratic form:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve the quadratic equation:**\n\n   We use the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -4 \\), and \\( c = -5 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-5)}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify under the square root:\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 20}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 \\pm 6}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   This yields two solutions:\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 + 6}{2} = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{4 - 6}{2} = -1\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sum the solutions:**\n\n   The \\( x \\)-values that satisfy the equation are \\( x = 5 \\) and \\( x = -1 \\).\n\n   Sum these solutions:\n\n   \\[\n   5 + (-1) = 4\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe sum of the \\( x \\)-values that satisfy the given equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
